   "reply": "{\"action\": \"finish\"}\n\nI fixed `days_between` in `dates.py`. The old `_ordinal` used `y * 365` and never counted leap days, so any span crossing a February in a leap year came out a day short.\n\nThe new version counts the leap days in all full years before the given year using the Gregorian rule (divisible by 4, except centuries not divisible by 400). It also adds one day for dates after February in a leap year.\n\nI checked it against Python's `datetime` on 20,000 random date pairs from 1900 onward, and all of them matched. The specific cases also came out right: 2024-02-28 to 2024-03-01 gives 2, 2000-02-28 to 2000-03-01 gives 2, and 1900-02-28 to 1900-03-01 gives 1, since 1900 wasn't a leap year.",
